#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1349
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuring With Document Variables"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1351
#, no-c-format
msgid "&kate; variables is katepart's implementation of document variables, similar to emacs and vi modelines. In katepart, the lines have the following format: <userinput>kate: VARIABLENAME VALUE; [ VARIABLENAME VALUE; ... ]</userinput> The lines can of course be in a comment, if the file is in a format with comments. Variable names are single words (no whitespace), and anything up to the next semicolon is the value. The semicolon is required."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1360
#, no-c-format
msgid "Here is an example variable line, forcing indentation settings for a C++, java or javascript file:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1363
#, no-c-format
msgid "// kate: space-indent on; indent-width 4; mixedindent off; indent-mode cstyle;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1366
#, no-c-format
msgid "Only the first and last 10 lines are searched for variable lines."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1368
#, no-c-format
msgid "Additionally, document variables can be placed in a file called <filename>.kateconfig</filename> in any directory, and the configured settings will be applied as if the modelines were entered on every file in the directory and its subdirectories, as far down as the <link linkend=\"pref-open-save-advanced\">configured search depth.</link> Document variables in <filename>.kateconfig</filename> use the same syntax as in modelines."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1376
#, no-c-format
msgid "There are variables to support almost all configurations in katepart, and additionally plugins can use variables, in which case it should be documented in the plugin's documentation."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1381
#, no-c-format
msgid "How &kate; uses Variables"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1383
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When reading configuration, katepart looks in the following places (in that order): "
"<itemizedlist> "
"<listitem>"
"<para>The global configuration.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" "
"<listitem>"
"<para>Optional session data.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" "
"<listitem>"
"<para>The \"Filetype\" configuration.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" "
"<listitem>"
"<para>Document variables in <filename>.kateconfig</filename>.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" "
"<listitem>"
"<para>Document variables in the document itself.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" "
"<listitem>"
"<para>Settings made during editing from menu or command line.</para>"
"</listitem>"
" </itemizedlist>"
" As you see, document variables are only overriden by changes made at runtime. Whenever a document is saved, the document variables are reread, and will overwrite changes made using menu items or the command line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1399
#, no-c-format
msgid "Any variable not listed below is stored in the document and can be queried by other objects such as plugins, which can use them for their own purpose. For example, the variable indent mode uses document variables for its configuration."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1404
#, no-c-format
msgid "The variables listed here documents &kate; version 3.7. More variables may be added in the future. There are 3 possible types of values for variables, with the following valid expressions:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#. +> trunk stable
#: configuring.docbook:1408
#, no-c-format
msgid "BOOL - on|off|true|false|1|0"
msgstr ""

